QR-Driven Warehouse Workflow Plan
Inventory Model: Model B — Stock Unit (Batch/Container)
The system tracks quantity-based inventory, not individual serialized units. A QR code identifies a stock unit — a scannable container, pallet, or batch at a specific location.


Stock Unit QR
├── Product (SKU)
├── Lot number (if tracked)
└── Quantity
The system holds the current location. Labels never need to change when a container moves — the system records the move.

Three QR Types
QR Type	Identifies	Generated when
Stock Unit QR	A batch/pallet/box (product + lot + qty)	At receipt post time
Location QR	A shelf, bin, dock bay, or area	When the location is created in the layout
Product QR	A SKU only	External — already on product packaging
QR Generation Rules
Stock Unit QRs are generated when a receipt is posted — one per receipt line
The QR encodes: product + lot + source receipt + original quantity
The QR does not encode location — location is held in the system and changes with every move
Location QRs are generated when a location is created in the warehouse layout and never regenerate
Product QRs are external — supplier barcodes read during receiving, never generated by the system
Workflow 1: Receiving
Purpose: Register incoming goods, assign them to a dock location, and print Stock Unit QR labels.

Scan-driven steps:

Open a new Receipt
Scan Location QR of the dock bay → sets the destination for all lines in this receipt
For each incoming container:
Scan Product QR (supplier barcode) → product is identified
Enter quantity (physical count — always manual)
Scan or type lot number if the product tracks lots
Post the receipt
System generates a Stock Unit QR label per line → worker prints and attaches to the container
Manual fallback: No product QR → search by SKU or name. No dock QR → select location from list.

Inventory change: Stock created at dock location. Each container is now scannable and visible on the GIS map.

Workflow 2: Putaway
Purpose: Move containers from dock to their storage location.

Scan-driven steps:

Scan Location QR of the destination shelf → sets the target location
Scan Stock Unit QR on the container → source location and product are identified
Confirm quantity (defaults to full container — edit only for partial putaway)
Confirm → move recorded
Repeat from step 2 for the next container going to this shelf
To switch destination: scan a new Location QR and continue
Manual fallback: No Stock Unit QR → search by product + lot. No shelf QR → select from location list.

Inventory change: Stock moves from dock to storage. GIS map reflects new position immediately.

Workflow 3: Internal Move
Purpose: Relocate a container between storage locations (reorganization, overflow, quarantine).

Scan-driven steps:

Scan Stock Unit QR → product, lot, and current location are identified
Scan destination Location QR → destination is set
Confirm quantity (defaults to full container — edit for partial move)
Confirm → move recorded
Manual fallback: Search by product + lot for source. Select destination from list.

Inventory change: Quantity decreases at source, increases at destination. Moving to quarantine is identical — quarantine is just a location with kind = QUARANTINE.

Workflow 4: Dispatch
Purpose: Remove goods from storage for an outgoing shipment.

Scan-driven steps:

Open a new Dispatch, optionally enter a reference (destination, order number)
Scan Stock Unit QR → product, lot, source location, and full quantity are pre-filled
Edit quantity if only a partial pick
Repeat steps 2–3 for each container being dispatched
Post the dispatch → stock removed from all source locations
Manual fallback: Search by product + lot if label is damaged. Select source location manually.

Inventory change: Stock removed from locations. Items no longer appear on the GIS map.

Workflow 5: Inventory Count
Purpose: Walk the floor and scan every container physically present. The system does the comparison — the worker just scans what they see.

Two Modes
Mode	When to use
Scan-first (default)	Worker walks the aisle scanning containers as fast as possible
Line-review	After scanning, worker reviews flagged discrepancies before posting
Phase 1: Open the Session
Open a new Count Session
Select which locations to include — scan Location QRs or select from the layout
System locks in the expected stock snapshot at that moment — this is the baseline for comparison
Session is open and ready for scanning
Phase 2: Scan a Location
Walk to a shelf
Scan the Location QR → app switches context to that location
Every container scanned next is counted at this location
Phase 3: Scan Containers
For each container physically on the shelf:

Scan the Stock Unit QR → system finds the matching expected line and increments its counted quantity by the container's known quantity
No confirmation step — each scan is instant
Scanning the same container twice: system warns and ignores the duplicate
Unknown container (not expected at this location):

Scan creates a new line with expected = 0 and counted = scanned quantity
Flagged immediately as unexpected stock
This includes containers the system expects to be at a different location
Damaged or missing label:

Worker taps Can't scan → searches by product + lot
Selects the matching stock unit
Optionally taps Reprint Label → prints a replacement Stock Unit QR → worker attaches it and scans it to confirm
System counts the unit normally — the reprint creates no inventory event
To move to the next shelf: scan a new Location QR and continue scanning.

Phase 4: End the Location Count
When the worker taps Done with this shelf, the system produces an immediate summary for that location:

Flag	Meaning
Matched	Scanned quantity = expected quantity
Discrepancy	Scanned quantity ≠ expected quantity
Missing	Expected containers that were never scanned
Unexpected	Scanned containers with no expected line here
The worker sees this before moving on. No action required — discrepancies are resolved at post time.

Phase 5: Post the Session
Worker reviews the full session summary across all counted locations
Taps Post
System generates ADJUST events only for lines where counted ≠ expected
Condition	Event
Counted > expected, or unexpected container found	ADJUST (positive)
Counted < expected, or expected container missing	ADJUST (negative)
Counted = expected	No event
GIS map updates to reflect corrected stock
Skipped locations: if a location was never scanned, its lines are left unchanged — no assumptions made.

Manual Fallback (No QR Available)
Select location manually from the layout
Search by product + lot for each container
Enter quantity manually
System behavior at post time is identical — this is just slower data entry
Count Rules
A container can only be counted once per session — duplicate scans rejected
A container found at the wrong location is counted as unexpected at the scanned location, and missing at its registered location
Reprinting a label during a count is allowed and creates no inventory event
Abandoning the session creates no events — nothing is committed until post
No approval step — the worker who counts also posts
Reprint QR
Labels get damaged. This is a maintenance operation, not an inventory operation.

Steps:

Scan or manually find the stock unit
Tap Reprint Label
System reprints the identical Stock Unit QR — same product, lot, quantity, no new record created
Worker removes the old label and attaches the new one
Rule: Reprint creates no inventory event. The stock unit itself is unchanged. This operation is available during receiving, putaway, any move, and count sessions.

Event Model
Event	Triggered by	Spatial meaning
RECEIVE	Posting a receipt line	Stock appears at dock location
MOVE	Any putaway or internal move	Stock changes location — stores from + to
PICK	Posting a dispatch line	Stock removed from location
ADJUST	Posting a count session (variance lines only)	Quantity corrected at a location
Every event stores: product, lot, location(s), quantity, timestamp, user, source document ID.

Note on current implementation: The live system records TRANSFER_OUT and TRANSFER_IN as two paired events per move. This is functionally equivalent to a single MOVE event. For GIS queries, the two sides of a move are already linked by a shared referenceId and can be treated as one logical event.

