Feature Development Plan
QR-Driven Warehouse System
Each feature is split into Backend (API + DB + migrations) and Frontend (UI + interactions). Features marked [EXISTS] are already implemented in the current codebase — they are listed for completeness and to define what future features build on.

F1 — Warehouse Layout & Location QR
Backend
Warehouse layout entity (name, description, tenant-scoped) [EXISTS]
Location/block entity with hierarchical structure (zone → aisle → shelf → bin) [EXISTS]
Location kind entity (DOCK, STORAGE, STAGING, QUARANTINE — user-defined strings) [EXISTS]
scanCode field on every location, generated at creation time, never changes [EXISTS]
API: create layout, create location, assign location kind [EXISTS]
API: resolve a scanned location QR → return location details [EXISTS]
Migration: ensure every location has a non-null scan_code unique per tenant
Frontend
Layout management page: create layouts, add locations in a tree structure [EXISTS]
Location kind management: define and name location kinds [EXISTS]
Location detail view showing its QR code (printable) [EXISTS]
Bulk QR print: select multiple locations, print a sheet of location labels
F2 — Product Catalog
Backend
Product entity: SKU, name, base UoM, track_lot flag, track_expiry flag, active flag [EXISTS]
Unit of measure entity [EXISTS]
API: CRUD for products and UoMs [EXISTS]
Product QR is external (supplier barcode) — system reads it, never generates it [EXISTS]
Frontend
Product management page: create, edit, deactivate [EXISTS]
UoM management page [EXISTS]
Product flags visible on the product form (track lot, track expiry) [EXISTS]
F3 — Receiving & Stock Unit QR
Backend
Receipt entity: reference, supplier, notes, status (DRAFT → POSTED → VOID), tenant-scoped [EXISTS]
Receipt line entity: product, location, quantity, lot, expiry [EXISTS]
Post logic: for each line, create stock at location and emit a RECEIVE event [EXISTS]
source_document_id on every generated movement points to the receipt [EXISTS]
Stock Unit QR payload: encodes { receiptId, lineId, productId, lotNumber, originalQty } — generated per line at post time [EXISTS]
API: create receipt, add/edit/remove lines, post, void [EXISTS]
API: get movements for a specific receipt [EXISTS]
Frontend
Receipt list page with status filter [EXISTS]
Receipt detail: add lines by scanning Product QR or searching by SKU [EXISTS]
Scan dock Location QR to pre-fill destination for all lines [EXISTS]
After posting: display generated Stock Unit QR labels, one per line [EXISTS]
Print button per label and bulk print all labels for the receipt
Movement history section on posted receipts [EXISTS]
F4 — Putaway (Dock → Storage)
Backend
Putaway is a transfer operation — reuses the existing transfer API [EXISTS]
Input: source (from Stock Unit QR), destination (from Location QR), quantity
Emits TRANSFER_OUT at source + TRANSFER_IN at destination, linked by reference_id [EXISTS]
Validation: source location must have sufficient stock for the requested quantity
Frontend
Dedicated Putaway screen optimized for scan-first flow:
Step 1: Scan destination Location QR → shelf is set as context
Step 2: Scan Stock Unit QR → source, product, lot, quantity are pre-filled
Step 3: Edit quantity only if partial putaway (defaults to full container)
Step 4: Confirm → move recorded → ready to scan next container
Persistent destination context: shelf stays set until worker scans a new Location QR
Manual fallback: search by product + lot for source, select destination from location tree
F5 — Internal Move
Backend
Same transfer API as putaway [EXISTS]
No structural difference from putaway at the data layer
Validation: same stock sufficiency check
Frontend
Move screen (simpler than putaway — no persistent destination context needed):
Scan Stock Unit QR → source, product, lot, quantity pre-filled
Scan destination Location QR → destination set
Edit quantity for partial moves
Confirm → move recorded
Manual fallback: search source by product + lot, select destination from list
Available from the stock detail view as a quick action ("Move this item")
F6 — Dispatch
Backend
Dispatch entity: reference, destination, notes, status (DRAFT → POSTED → VOID) [EXISTS]
Dispatch line entity: product, source location, quantity, lot, notes [EXISTS]
Post logic: for each line, reduce stock at source location and emit a PICK event [EXISTS]
Validation at post: each line must have sufficient stock at source location
source_document_id on every generated movement points to the dispatch [EXISTS]
API: create dispatch, add/edit/remove lines, post, void [EXISTS]
API: get movements for a specific dispatch [EXISTS]
Frontend
Dispatch list page with status filter [EXISTS]
Dispatch detail: add lines by scanning Stock Unit QR
Scanning a Stock Unit QR pre-fills product, lot, source location, and full quantity
Worker edits quantity for partial picks
Manual fallback: search by product + lot, select source location manually [EXISTS]
Post confirmation with stock validation feedback (e.g. "insufficient stock at location X")
Movement history section on posted dispatches [EXISTS]
F7 — Inventory Count (Scan-First)
Backend
Count session entity: name, status (OPEN → POSTED → VOID), tenant-scoped [EXISTS]
Count line entity: location, product, lot, expected_qty, counted_qty, flag [EXISTS]
flag field (new): enum — MATCHED, DISCREPANCY, MISSING, UNEXPECTED
Session open: snapshot current stock for selected locations into expected lines [EXISTS]
Scan increment endpoint (new): accepts a Stock Unit QR scan during an open session
Finds matching expected line (same product + lot at scanned location)
Increments counted_qty by the container's known quantity
If no matching expected line: creates a new line with expected_qty = 0, flags as UNEXPECTED
If the scanned container is registered at a different location: flag as UNEXPECTED here and MISSING at its registered location
Duplicate scan detection: reject if the same Stock Unit QR has already been counted in this session
Location summary endpoint (new): returns all lines for one location with their current flags
Post logic: for each line where counted_qty ≠ expected_qty, emit an ADJUST event [EXISTS]
Positive ADJUST if counted > expected
Negative ADJUST if counted < expected
Zero-variance lines produce no events [EXISTS]
Skipped locations (no lines touched): no events generated, no assumptions made
API: open session, scan container, update line manually (fallback), post, void [EXISTS partial]
API: get movements for a specific count session [EXISTS]
Migration: add flag column to count lines, add scanned_unit_ids set per session for duplicate detection
Frontend
Count session list page [EXISTS]
Scan-first count screen (primary new UI):
Scan Location QR → app locks to that shelf, shows its expected lines in the background
Scan Stock Unit QR → matched line highlights and count increments instantly, no confirmation needed
Running tally visible: "X of Y containers scanned at this location"
Duplicate scan: brief warning toast, scan ignored
"Done with this shelf" button → triggers location summary view
Location summary view (new):
Shows all lines for the just-counted location grouped by flag:
Matched (green)
Discrepancy (amber) — shows expected vs counted
Missing (red) — containers in the system not found on the shelf
Unexpected (amber) — containers found but not expected here
Worker can tap any line to correct manually if needed
"Continue to next shelf" button → back to scan mode
"Can't scan" flow (fallback during count):
Search by product + lot
Select matching stock unit
Optionally tap Reprint Label → print replacement → scan new label to confirm
System counts the unit normally
Session summary view (before posting):
Full breakdown of all flags across all counted locations
Uncounted locations listed separately (no changes will be made to them)
Post button
Manual quantity entry mode: if worker navigates to a line manually, they can type a counted quantity — this is the fallback for locations with no readable QR codes at all [EXISTS]
F8 — QR Scan Infrastructure
Backend
Scan resolver service: accepts a raw QR string, returns structured resolve result [EXISTS]
Resolves to: location, product, stock unit (receipt line), or unknown
Resolver handles all three QR types: Location QR, Stock Unit QR, Product QR [EXISTS]
Duplicate scan detection during count sessions (see F7)
Frontend
ScanInput component: camera-based QR reader with manual entry fallback [EXISTS]
Scan result is resolved immediately — no manual confirmation of the resolved entity needed
Error state for unrecognized QR codes
Scan sound/haptic feedback on successful resolve
The component is reusable across all workflows — receiving, putaway, move, dispatch, count
F9 — Label Reprint
Backend
Reprint endpoint: accepts a receipt line ID or a stock unit identifier
Returns the original QR payload — same content as generated at receipt post time
Creates no inventory event — purely a label operation
Available at any time, not tied to any session status
Audit log entry for the reprint action (who reprinted, when, which unit)
Frontend
Reprint button available in:
Receipt detail view (per line, for posted receipts)
Stock detail view (for any stock unit)
Count session scan screen (as part of the "Can't scan" fallback flow)
Triggers print dialog or sends to a configured label printer
No confirmation required — the operation is non-destructive
F10 — Inventory Visibility
Backend
Stock query API: returns current stock, filterable by product, location, location kind, lot [EXISTS]
Movements API: paginated movement history, filterable by product, location, source document, movement type [EXISTS]
Stock enrichment: every stock entry includes location label, path, kind, product name, SKU, UoM [EXISTS]
Movement enrichment: every movement includes counterpart location (for transfers) and source document reference [EXISTS]
Frontend
Inventory page with tabs: Stock, Movements, Operations [EXISTS]
Stock tab: filter by product, location, and location kind [EXISTS]
Movements tab: filter by product and location; source document chip (Receipt / Dispatch / Count Session) linking to the originating document [EXISTS]
Operations tab: receive, transfer, and adjust operations for manual corrections [EXISTS]
Movement history section embedded in posted Receipts, Dispatches, and Count Sessions [EXISTS]
Implementation Order (Suggested)
Phase	Features	Reason
1	F1, F2, F8	Foundation — everything depends on locations, products, and scan infrastructure
2	F3	Receiving creates stock and generates Stock Unit QRs — required before any movement
3	F4, F5, F6	Movement and dispatch workflows — depend on stock existing
4	F10	Visibility — more useful once real stock exists from phases 2–3
5	F7, F9	Counting and reprint — operational tools that require the full stock lifecycle to exist first
